[HowItWorksText]
<h2>About this disassembly</h2>
<p>This is a complete annotated disassembly of the main code block of the original 1982 release (v1.0) of <i>The Hobbit</i> by Veronika Megler and Philip Mitchell, published by Melbourne House. The block is 37,888 bytes long, loads at 24576 (#R$6000) and is started by <tt>RANDOMIZE USR 27648</tt>. Reassembling the disassembly with SkoolKit gives back the original block byte for byte.</p>
<p>The code and data were identified by running the game in a Z80 emulator, by tracing every table and pointer, and by hand. Many descriptions were confirmed by playing the relevant scene in the emulator; the Trivia page lists what was checked this way. Everything else is a careful reading of the code.</p>
<p>Besides the routine and data pages, see: <a href="Map.html">the map of Wilderland</a>, <a href="Pictures.html">the location pictures</a>, <a href="Characters.html">the characters</a>, <a href="Objects.html">the objects</a>, <a href="reference/facts.html">trivia</a> and <a href="reference/bugs.html">bugs</a>.</p>

<h2>Memory map</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Address</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
<tr><td>#R$6000($6000)</td><td>Dictionary letter index, main dictionary (the words the player can type, with their classes and synonyms) and message dictionary</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$6C00($6C00)</td><td>Start-up, main loop, keyboard and line input, tokeniser, message interpreter, text output, 6-pixel font (#R$7815)</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$7C4F($7C4F)</td><td>Parser, game commands (HELP, SCORE, SAVE, LOAD, PRINT...), command execution</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$8965($8965)</td><td>Picture drawing</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$8C2D($8C2D)</td><td>Action handlers: LOOK, TAKE, DROP, movement, fights, eating, doors and containers...</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$9611($9611)</td><td>End of turn: timers and the independent characters; object and room utilities</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$A174($A174)</td><td>Special handlers for particular objects, characters, rooms and timers</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$AA47($AA47)</td><td>The action table (the 59 things that can be done)</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$AC31($AC31)</td><td>Message abbreviations and the messages</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$B5CB($B5CB)</td><td>Game variables, verb endings, orders buffer and command frames</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$B8D0($B8D0)</td><td>Room pointers and room records</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$BF53($BF53)</td><td>Object index and object records (everything, including Bilbo and the characters)</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$C61F($C61F)</td><td>Default action handlers, room-entry handlers, riddles, hidden routes, behaviour programs, timers, character table</td></tr>
<tr><td>#R$CC00($CC00)</td><td>Location pictures</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>A turn</h2>
<p>The main loop (#R$6D0A) reads a line of typing (#R$6DCD). If the player does nothing for about 20 seconds the game types WAIT itself (#R$7240), so the world goes on without him. The tokeniser (#R$6E8E) looks each word up in the dictionary, allowing abbreviations and replacing synonyms (HIT, KILL and CAPTURE all become ATTACK). The parser (#R$7C4F) builds command frames: a verb, a particle and two noun phrases each, several per line, with AND, THEN, ALL, EXCEPT, IT and quoted orders to other characters.</p>
<p>Each frame is matched against the action table (#R$858F) and the objects that fit its words are tried one by one in silent 'dry runs' until one works (#R$8405); if that is ambiguous the game asks 'which key?' and lets the reply finish the command. The action is then performed (#R$946F) by the object's own handler if it has one, or by a default handler, and reported ('you open the door.', 'thorin attacks you.'). Then the turn ends (#R$9611): every other character acts (#R$976C) and the ten timers tick (#R$C973).</p>

<h2>The world</h2>
<p>Everything that exists is an object with a 16-byte header (#R$C00B): Bilbo is object 0, and characters, doors, rivers and swords all share the same format - size, weight, strength, defence, side, flags, four words, locations, and a list of its own action handlers. Being inside, on, tied to or carried by something is just a matter of whose number is in the 'held by' byte. The 79 rooms (#R$B97A) have a name, an optional description, a preposition, light, and exits that may pass through doors (see the <a href="Map.html">map</a>). All of it - objects, rooms, variables, timers, characters - is copied aside at start-up and copied back to restart the game, and exactly those areas are what SAVE writes to tape.</p>

<h2>The characters</h2>
<p>Each character runs a little program of its own (#R$C71C): do an action, move, call a special routine, jump, react to what is done to it. They use the same action routines as the player, which is why the game can say 'gandalf opens the round green door' or 'thorin sits down and starts singing about gold' with the same code that handles Bilbo. They can be given orders with SAY TO, which they may refuse (#R$8F9E). Some are asleep until Bilbo gets near: the butler, Bard and the dragon (#R$C693, #R$C6AF).</p>

<h2>Text</h2>
<p>Messages are programs (#R$72D4): dictionary words, one-byte abbreviations for common words, and control codes for 'the actor', 'his or your', 'is or are', jumps and sub-messages. Verbs get their endings automatically when the subject is not 'you' (#R$74B8), articles come from flags in each word reference (#R$7436), and capital letters are added only by the printer (#R$7694), which also wraps words and slows down long bursts of text. The story window uses a 6-pixel font (#R$77BC), and the input window the ROM font.</p>

<h2>Pictures</h2>
<p>The 22 location pictures (#R$CC00) are drawn at run time from a tiny vector language - moves, lines with fixed slopes, flood fills and blocks of colour - by #R$8985. Two of them borrow the drawing of another picture by running on into it.</p>

<h2>Chance</h2>
<p>The random number generator (#R$9BFD) takes its seed from the R register when the first key is pressed and then reads bytes of the program itself; its way of folding numbers into a range skews them towards the high end. Chance decides fights, which route is closed and which riddle Gollum asks, whether characters obey, and much of what they do.</p>

[Fact:eyes:The pale bulbous eyes]
Entering the forest road (room 2) or the forest (room 3) runs the room-entry handler #R$C6CC, which starts timer 8 (#R$C973) at 4 and remembers the room.
<br><br>
At the end of each turn the count goes down by one. While it is 3, 2 or 1 the tick routine #R$AA13 prints 'you see some pale bulbous eyes staring at you' and checks that Bilbo is still in the room he entered; if he has left (other than to the other forest room) something drops from above and stings him to death. When it reaches 0 the expire routine #R$AA2E kills Bilbo if he is still in room 2 or 3, and does nothing if he has gone.
<br><br>
So on entering, the count drops to 3 at the end of that turn; two WAITs take it to 2 and then 1; and moving on the third turn takes it to 0 with Bilbo already gone. Any command that uses a turn counts as a wait. Walking back and forth between rooms 2 and 3 simply restarts the timer.

[Fact:fight:How a fight is decided]
The attack value is the attacker's strength (object byte 5) plus the weapon's strength, capped at 255; the defence is the target's byte 6. Both are randomised by #R$917D. If the defence is at least the attack, the blow is wasted; if the attack wins by more than 16, the target is killed; otherwise it is wounded, with a message chosen by the margin (#R$9190) and some loss of strength and defence. See #R$90DB for the details.
<br><br>
With the original sword (strength 64) Bilbo attacks at 128 against Thorin's defence of 120, so most blows wound him. Bare-handed Bilbo attacks at 64 and almost never gets through, except when the bug in #R$917D reduces Thorin's defence to 0. Thorin's own attack (104) against Bilbo's defence (64) is nearly always fatal.

[Fact:characters:Independent characters]
The characters in the table at #R$C9BA act on their own every turn (#R$976C), using the very same routines as the player, which is why messages read 'thorin attacks you' or 'gandalf opens the round green door'. Their behaviour involves random choices, so every game is different - and Thorin's habit of sitting down and singing about gold comes from this system.

[Bug:negativeRandom:Negative random adjustments become zero]
In #R$917D a random adjustment of -1 to -10 is added to a fight value. The code uses the carry flag to detect overflow, but adding a negative (two's complement) number to a value larger than its size always sets the carry, so the routine wrongly concludes that the result has overflowed and returns 0. About 4% of adjustments are negative, so about 4% of attack or defence values collapse to zero. A zeroed defence makes the next blow almost certainly fatal, which explains many of the game's sudden deaths.

[Bug:woundTable:Missing wound message]
The wound message table at #R$9190 has no valid entry for a margin of exactly 16. The two bytes read instead are code at #R$91B0, giving a message address of $2ADD in the ROM, so the game prints garbage.

[Bug:rrca:Wound damage computed with RRCA]
The routine at #R$90DB halves the margin with RRCA instead of SRL. RRCA feeds the bit that falls off the bottom back in at the top, so for many margins the 'halved' value is over 128 and the reduction is either skipped or enormous. For Thorin, margins 3, 7, 11 and 15 do no damage at all, while margins 1, 5, 9 and 13 cut his defence from 120 to about 53 in one blow.

[Poke:swordCarried:Start the game carrying the sword]
POKE 49653,0 (the sword is held by object 0, Bilbo) and POKE 49668,1 (and it is in room 1, with him).

[Poke:swordStrength:The mightiest sword]
POKE 49657,255 (the sword's strength becomes 255, so Bilbo attacks at the maximum of 255).

[Glossary:Actor]
The character currently performing an action: Bilbo (object 0) or one of the independent characters. Held in #R$B5DB.

[Glossary:Dry run]
A trial execution of a command routine to find out whether it would succeed, with no visible effects. See #R$9C99.

[Glossary:Word reference]
A 12-bit offset from #R$6000 identifying a word in one of the dictionaries, often stored in two bytes with flag bits in the top four.
